[Music]  did you know that just this year  paleontologists have discovered new  insights into the behavior and  appearance of spinosaurus the  biomechanics of megalodon teeth in the  strangest early life forms of the  cambrian explosion new revelations are  occurring all the time regarding earth's  distant past yet these incredible  findings often go unnoticed by the  general public  so this year i'm taking part in paleo  rewind a breakdown of some of the most  important revelations in the field of  paleontology from the past year all  sorts of other creators you might  already be subscribed to are also  participating in this video i'll kick  the rewind off by covering findings from  the first half of january but be sure to  check out everyone else's entries too in  the final 2021 compilation video that  will be uploaded on edge's channel now  let's get started  beginning in early january 2021 we have  new findings on the potential ecology  and feeding behavior of the famous  theropod spinosaurus the spinosaurus is  a dinosaur that has long gripped popular  culture in its elongated jaws thanks in  part to its appearance in the jurassic  park franchise but the common conception  i grew up with of the spinosaurus as a  bigger heavier t-rex analog isn't quite  accurate indeed the spinosaurus has been  the subject of considerable recent  debate regarding its biology and ecology  as it might have been highly unique from  other carnivorous theropods due to its  semi-aquatic or perhaps highly aquatic  lifestyle a paper published january 7th  by dr david hohn and dr thomas holtz  weighs the likelihood of spinosaurus  being a waiting animal that fed on fish  near the shorelines and in shallow water  or a specialized aquatic pursuit  predator their studies were quite  complex but to simplify based on the  anatomy of the fossils we have and  related ecological data the waiting  model is more supported than the highly  aquatic one still the authors note that  spinosaurus almost certainly fed  primarily from the water and may have  swum which alone might have made it  incredibly distinct from most other  large theropods  our next discovery exposes new  revelations regarding the history of a  highly adapted group of aquatic  predators sharks for hundreds of  millions of years sharks of various  sizes have been patrolling earth's  oceans and throughout the cenozoic an  extinct family known as megatooth sharks  started to trend towards gigantism a  trend that culminated in the well-known  megalodon the largest macro predatory  shark to ever exist this species  exceeded lengths of up to 15 meters or  50 feet and likely weighed more than 50  tons but a mystery has long surrounded  the megalodon regarding the nature of  their teeth  compared to the teeth of earlier  megatooth sharks megalodon teeth  consistently had greater crown width for  reasons that aren't entirely clear  recently dr antonio bael and dr humberto  ferrone ran a series of experiments  testing the biomechanics of earlier  megatooth shark teeth compared to the  megalodon and the results were quite  surprising it seemed stress distribution  patterns were remarkably similar across  all models meaning that the teeth of  megalodons while obviously deadly  weren't any better adapted than the  teeth of other sharks in their family it  seems that megalodon teeth shifted in  shape as a byproduct of their gigantism  instead of a specific evolutionary  advantage still it wouldn't have been  fun to get bitten by one  winding back the clock by half a billion  years we reached the cambrian explosion  a metaphorical eruption in the diversity  of early life forms which as of january  2021 we have new information on you  might already know that the cambrian  explosion gave rise to countless bizarre  life forms many of which seem more like  alien life than something from earth's  past from the surreal opinion which  might have possessed a flexible trunk  that makes it look almost like a living  cartoon to the hallucigenia an animal so  dreamlike its name comes from the word  hallucination it's a fascinating early  milestone in earth's biodiversity in a  recent examination by dr aj shore and dr  r.a wood unveils a new addition to this  pantheon of oddities through the  analysis of some exceptionally  well-preserved fossils researchers were  able to distinguish a life form that  possessed a cup-shaped skeleton with six  radially arranged lobes surrounding what  was likely its mouth a reconstruction of  this life form shows it was a highly  unique form of animal in perhaps a  foundational member of the  lofotrochozoan clade a group that today  includes various types of cephalopods  and mollusks  sometimes seemingly small discoveries in  the realm of paleontology can lead to  major revelations one article from  january 6 shows a curious potential  relationship between herbivorous  dinosaurs and the very plants that they  ate a new examination of fossilized gut  contents by dr george perry suggests  plant seeds remained intact within the  stomachs of plant-eating dinosaurs  cross-referencing this data with the  distances they likely walked it seems  possible that herbivorous dinosaurs  disperse plant seeds far and wide via  their well droppings strange though this  might sound some modern plant munchers  like african bush elephants play a  similar role in the  seed dispersal of certain types of  plants in fact the dispersal of plant  seeds within the bodies of animals is so  frequent in current ecosystems that  plants often modify their fruits and  flowers to appeal more to specific  species and since some dinosaurs could  consume even more plant matter per day  than elephants it's likely they were  highly sought after seed distributors  millions of years ago a strange but  fascinating discovery  and our last article covers a whole new  taxon of marine predator with  specialized teeth unlike those of any  known reptile this new taxon is a member  of the mosasaurids a group of  mid-cretaceous lizards that became  specialized for marine life this  exciting new taxon called xenodens  kalmanchari lived just before the kpg  extinction and stands out from all other  mosasaurids as reported in the article  by dr natalie barde and dr nicholas  longrich this new mosasaurid possessed  teeth that were hooked and formed a  saw-like blade the specialized teeth  suggest a previously unknown hunting  strategy likely involving a cutting  motion to carve pieces out of large prey  or perhaps these unique teeth were used  for scavenging already deceased marine  lifeforms  in either case these teeth are unique  not just among mosasaurids but all  reptiles in general  and those are just some of the major  paleontological revelations of early  january special thanks to edge for  inviting me to be a part of paleo rewind  other channels like mine will be  releasing videos covering other months  in the coming days and i'll add links to  them in the description when they upload  and paleoanalysis is covering the second  half of january so check out their great  video next if you want to go in order  and as always thanks for watching if you  enjoyed this entry into the archive  please lend your support and like  subscribe and hit the notification icon  to stay up to date on all things curious  see you in the next video  [Music]  you

